### Trimming, indexing and padding (R23).

These three were refused by the backend for the same reason as the case
mappings: the interpreter's are JavaScript's, and JavaScript indexes and
measures strings in UTF-16 while this language counts code points. Two were
settled by saying which unit wins.

`index_of` gives a code-point index and `pad_*` count code points, which is
what `length` and `chars` already do. A string method that measured in one
unit and indexed in another would be wrong in a way that only showed up on
somebody else's alphabet -- and could not be compiled, because a unit this
language does not have is not a unit C can count in.

`trim` needed nothing settled. The set of characters it removes is
twenty-five code points long, and short enough to write out and read.

Still refused: `upper` and `lower`, whose answers come out of Unicode case
tables.
###
main(),
    # Trimming, including the whitespace that is not a space.
    say "  padded  ".trim(),
    say "  padded  ".trim_start(),
    say "  padded  ".trim_end(),
    say "\tmixed\n".trim(),
    say "\u{00a0}nbsp\u{00a0}".trim(),
    say "\u{2003}emsp\u{2003}".trim(),
    say "\u{feff}bom\u{feff}".trim(),
    say "nothing to trim".trim(),
    say "".trim(),
    say "   ".trim(),
    say "   ".trim().is_empty(),

    # A character that is *not* whitespace must survive, including ones
    # that look like it: U+200B is a zero-width space and JavaScript does
    # not trim it, so neither does this.
    say "\u{200b}zwsp\u{200b}".trim().length,

    # Trimming leaves the middle alone.
    say "  a  b  ".trim(),

    # Indexing, in code points.
    say "hello".index_of("l"),
    say "hello".index_of("z"),
    say "hello".index_of(""),
    say "hello".index_of("hello"),

    # The part before the match is multi-byte, so a byte index would say 3
    # and a UTF-16 index would say 2. Code points say 2.
    say "héllo".index_of("l"),
    say "aé😀b".index_of("b"),
    say "aé😀b".length,

    # Padding, in code points.
    say "7".pad_start(3),
    say "7".pad_end(3),
    say "7".pad_start(3, "0"),
    say "ab".pad_start(6, "12"),
    say "ab".pad_end(6, "12"),

    # A filler wider than the gap is cut, and the cut is on a character.
    say "ab".pad_start(5, "xyz"),
    say "ab".pad_end(5, "xyz"),

    # Already long enough, so nothing happens.
    say "abcdef".pad_start(3),
    say "abcdef".pad_end(3),

    # And the case the UTF-16 count gets wrong: one emoji is one code
    # point, so this needs two more to reach three.
    say "😀".pad_start(3, "-"),
    say "😀".pad_start(3, "-").length,
    say "é".pad_end(4, "."),
    say "é".pad_end(4, ".").length,

    # Built rather than written, so the strings carry counts to give back.
    let acc: "",
    for i in 0..6,
        acc: acc + "{i}",
    say acc.pad_start(10, ".").trim(),
    say acc.index_of("3")
